","To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we use the Beer-Lambert's Law:

\[
\text{ΔH = Σ[H_f(products)] - Σ[H_f(reactants)]
\]

Where:
- ΔH is the standard enthalpy change
- H_f is the standard enthalpy of formation

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The balanced equation shows one mole of CaO reacting with one mole of H2O to produce one mole of Ca(OH)2.

The standard enthalpy of formation for CaO(s) is -635.1 kg/mol.
The standard enthalpy of formation for H2O(l) is -285.8 kg/mol.
The standard enthalpy of formation for Ca(OH)2(s) is -986.2 kg/mol.

Now, we can calculate the standard enthalpy change:

\[
\text{ΔH = H_f(Ca(OH)_2(s)) - [H_f(CaO(s)) + H_f(H_2O(l))]
\]
\[
\text{ΔH = (-986.2 kg/mol) - [(-635.1 kg/mol) + (-285.8 kg/mol)]
\]
\[
\text{ΔH = -986.2 kg/mol + 635.1 kg/mol + 285.8 kg/mol
\]
\[
\text{ΔH = -986.2 kg/mol + 920.9 kg/mol
\]
\[
\text{ΔH = -65.3 kg/mol
}\]

