","The equation of the line tangent to the graph of \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by the **Derivative Rule for Tangent Line Equation**:  
\[
f'(a) \cdot (x - a) + f(a) = y
\]  
This can also be written as:  
\[
y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
\]  
This equation is derived from the definition of the derivative as the slope of the tangent line at a point:  
\[
f'(a) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(a + h) - f(a)}{h) = \text{slope of the tangent line at } (a, f(a))
\]  
Using the point-slope form of a line, \( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \), where \( m \) is the slope and \( (x_1, y_1) \) is the point, we substitute \( m = f'(a) \) and \( (x_1, y_1) = (a, f(a)) \):  
\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]  
Rearranging gives the equation:  
\[
y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
\]  

Thus, the equation of the tangent line is:  
\[
\boxed{y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)}
\]",relevance,"# Task Description
