Relative speed = Speed of the train - Speed of the jogger
Speed of the train = 45 km/hr
Speed of the jogger = 9 km/hr

To convert the speeds to meters per second (m/s), we use the conversion factor of 1 km/hr = 1000 m/min = 1000/60 m/min = 100/6 m/s = 50/3 m/s.

Speed of the train in m/s = 45 * (50/3) = 15 * 50 = 750 m/s
Speed of the jogger in m/s = 9 * (50/3) = 3 * 50 = 150 m/s

Relative speed = 750 m/s - 150 m/s = 600 m/s

Now, we know that the train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. This means that in 25 seconds, the train covers the distance equal to the length of the train plus the initial distance between the train and the jogger.

Initial distance between the train and the jogger = 150 m

The length of the train can be found by multiplying the relative speed by the time it takes for the train to pass the jogger.

Length of the train = Relative speed * Time
Length of the train = 600 m/s * 25 s
Length of the train = 1500 m

Therefore, the length of the train is $\boxed{1500}$  meters.
