","To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction between hydrogen gas and oxygen gas to form water, we can use the Beer-Lambert equation:

\[
\Delta J = \sum \left( \Delta G_{f,H_{2}} \right) - \sum \left( \Delta G_{f,H_{2}} \right)
\]

For the reaction:
\[
2H_{2} + O_{2} \rightarrow 2H_{2}O
\]

The Gibbs free capacity of formation for the reactants and product are:
- For \( H_{2} \): \(\Delta G_{f} = 0 \) (since it is a pure element)
- For \( O_{2} \): \(\Delta G_{f} = 0 \) (since it is a pure element)
- For \( H_{2}O \): \(\Delta G_{f} = -237.1 \text{ kJ/mol} \)

Using the Beer-Lambert equation:
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times \left( \Delta G_{f} \text{ of } H_{2} \right) + 1 \times \left( \Delta G_{f} \text{ of } O_{2} \right) - 2 \times \left( \Delta G_{f} \text{ of } H_{2}O \right)
\]
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times 0 + 1 \times 0 - 2 \times (-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol})
\]
\[
\Delta G = 474.2 \text{ kJ}
\]

Thus, the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction is \(\boxed{474.2 \text{ kJ}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
