","To determine the minimum inlet pressure required to prevent cavitation, we need to compare the minimum pressure threshold for cavitation with the pressure at the inlet of the pump. The minimum pressure threshold is given by the minimum pressure drop threshold, which is 100°C.

1. **Determine the depth of the inlet pipe**:
   The inlet pipe is 5 meters below the water surface.

2. **Determine the ambient pressure at the inlet**:
   The ambient pressure at the inlet is the sum of the pressure due to the water surface and the pressure due to the depth of the inlet pipe.
   \[
   P_{\text{ambient}} = P_{\text{water source}} + P_{\text{depth}}
   \]
   The pressure due to the water surface is the height of the water surface (1 meter) times the density of the water (1000 kg/m$^3$) times the gravitational acceleration (9.81 m/s$^2$).
   \[
   P_{\text{water source}} = 1000 \times 9.81 \times 1 = 9810 \text{ J/m}^3
   \]
   The pressure due to the depth of the inlet pipe is the depth of the inlet pipe (5 m) times the density of the water (1000 kg/m$^3$) times the gravitational acceleration (9.81 m/s$^2$).
   \[
   P_{\text{depth}} = 1000 \times 9.81 \times 5 = 49050 \text{ J/m}^3
   \]
   The ambient pressure at the inlet is the sum of these two pressures:
   \[
   P_{\text{ambient}} = 9810 + 49050 = 58860 \text{ J/m}^3
   \]

