The uncertainty in the energy measurement is determined by the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, which in this case relates the uncertainty in the energy (\(\Delta E\)) to the lifetime (\(\Delta t\)) of the state:

\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\13}{2\pi \Delta t}
\]

Given the lifetime \(2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s, we can calculate the uncertainty in the energy:

\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{13}{2\pi \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23}\text{ s}} \approx 1.03 \times 10^{-22}\text{ eV}
\]

To convert this to MeV, we divide by \(10^{6}\):

\[
\Delta E \geq 1.03 \times 10^{-22} \times 10^{6}\text{ MeV} \approx 1.03 \times 10^{-16}\text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the uncertainty in the energy is \(1.03 \times 10^{-16}\text{ MeV}\).

The typical range of outcomes for the measurement of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be:

\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 1.03 \times 10^{-16}\text{ MeV}
\]

This uncertainty is extremely small, indicating that the measurement would be highly precise.
