## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
1. **Initial Closed-Loop Gain:**
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 + A_v m_v} = \frac{100}{1 + 100 \times 0.1} = \frac{100}{11} \approx 9.09
   \]

2. **New Gain After 6 dB Drop:**
   A 6 dB drop corresponds to halving the voltage gain:
   \[
   A_v' = \frac{A_v}{2} = \frac{100}{2} = 50
   \]

3. **New Closed-Loop Gain:**
   \[
   A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 + A_v' m_v} = \frac{50}{1 + 50 \times 0.1} = \frac{50}{6} \approx 8.33
   \]

4. **Percentage Change in Closed-Loop Gain:**
   \[
   \text{Percentage Change} = \frac{A_{vf} - A_{vf}'}{A_{vf}} \times 100 = \frac{9.09 - 8.33}{9.09} \times 100 \approx 8.36\%
   \]

   The closed-loop gain decreases by approximately \(\boxed{8.36\%}\).
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The forward voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \). The feedback ratio \( m_v = 0.1 \). The gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB, which is equivalent to a factor of \( 10^{-6/10} = 0.5 \). Thus, the new forward gain is \( A_v' = 0.5 \times 100 = 50 \).

The forward feedback gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v}
\]

Initially, the closed-loop gain is:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 111.11 \text{ V/V}
\]

After aging, the new feedback gain is:
\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 - m_v} = \frac{50}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{50}{0.9} \approx 55.56 \text{ V/V}
\]

The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:
\[
\text{Percentage Change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \frac{55.56 - 111.11}{111.11} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \frac{-55.55}{111.11} \right) \times 100\% \approx -50\%
\]

Thus, the percent change in the closed-loop gain is approximately \(\boxed{-50\%}\).
