","(a) The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is given by the Lorentz force equation:  
\[
F = qE
\]  
where \( F \) is the force, \( q \) is the charge, and \( E \) is the electric field. The acceleration \( a \) is then given by:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{qE}{m}
\]  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
a = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The time \( t \) it takes for the proton to reach a speed \( v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \) is given by the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = v_0 + at
\]  
Since the proton starts from rest, \( v_0 = 0 \). Solving for \( t \):  
\[
